zram: Compressed RAM based block devices
----------------------------------------
* Introduction

The zram module creates RAM based block devices named /dev/zram<id>
(<id> = 0, 1, ...). Pages written to these disks are compressed and stored
in memory itself. These disks allow very fast I/O and compression provides
good amounts of memory savings. Some of the usecases include /tmp storage,
use as swap disks, various caches under /var and maybe many more :)

Statistics for individual zram devices are exported through sysfs nodes at
/sys/block/zram<id>/

Kconfig required:
CONFIG_ZRAM=y
CONFIG_CRYPTO_LZ4=y
CONFIG_ZPOOL=y
CONFIG_ZSMALLOC=y

ZRAM Testcases
--------------
zram_lib.sh: create library with initialization/cleanup functions
zram.sh: For sanity check of CONFIG_ZRAM and to run zram01 and zram02

Two functional tests: zram01 and zram02:
zram01.sh: creates general purpose ram disks with ext4 filesystems
zram02.sh: creates block device for swap

Commands required for testing:
 - bc
 - dd
 - free
 - awk
 - mkswap
 - swapon
 - swapoff
 - mkfs/ mkfs.ext4

Host-only writeback regression tests
-----------------------------------
These targets are independent of the device-oriented run_tests target above:

  make -C tools/testing/selftests/zram host-test
  make -C tools/testing/selftests/zram host-test-sanitize

Or run python3 -I tools/testing/selftests/zram/zram_writeback_host_test.py,
adding --sanitize for AddressSanitizer/UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer. HOSTCC may
select an existing host compiler (default gcc). No downloads, /dev access,
sysfs writes, zram.sh, build.sh, or kernel build are invoked. Temporary files
are created under the host temporary directory and cleaned on exit.

The Python harness extracts the actual driver helpers on every run and reports
their SHA256. C mocks only kernel contracts (locks, memory, BIOs, usercopy,
clock/randomness, block device). It runs at simulated 4/16/64KiB PAGE_SIZE.
Missing implementation is KSFT_SKIP (4), not a passing stand-alone model.

Coverage retains the original six groups: reserve/refund units; flush errors,
partial grants and signals; slot free/replacement/read/ABA invalidation; batch
sizes 1/31/32/33/65 and backing gaps; legacy per-request installation bound;
allocation fallback and all old cleanup regressions. Six new groups execute
actual EXEC/STATUS/compat dispatch: independent request caps and exact counts,
unsubmitted/single-count invalidations and concurrent installed-page freeing,
error stages/first-cause precedence and retained partial effects, bytewise
reserved/output validation and permissions, sampled status/stale/lifecycle
cookie rollover, and native/compat partial copyout failure without refunds.
Reusing an ID is tested as a new operation, not deduplication.

Both real UAPI structs compile with -m32 and -m64, checking all member offsets,
alignment, size and ioctl numbers (no 32-bit libc needed). Feature-off native
and compat dispatch/fops compile and execute; feature-off shared engine runs
all six old groups at every page size. Lifecycle helpers execute with scripted
random cookies; source checks additionally cover initialization, backing
setup/removal, accepted reset's early return, and device removal call sites.

Limits: these are host simulations, not a kernel configuration build, real
concurrency/lockdep/KCSAN test, live reset/removal, physical I/O fault injection,
or deployment validation. 32-bit checks cover userspace ABI, not a 32-bit
kernel; the existing slot-flag layout is not changed in this phase. Full
kernel builds and device acceptance remain separate manual gates.

For more information please refer:
kernel-source-tree/Documentation/blockdev/zram.txt
